  "title": "María, España --- The Nouns That Need No Article",
  "drivablePrefix": 1,
  "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:d170c122b4cf33da",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "ES-C66-nombres-propios",
      "sequence": 2535,
      "title": "María, España, profesor — the nouns that need no article",
      "headword": "María · España · profesor",
      "romanization": "María · España · profesor",
      "gloss": "names, places and titles — the nouns that behave differently from every other noun, and the article rule that follows from it",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:dafdba0ece34adb6",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "El libro, la casa. The article's job is to say which one."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Now try putting one in front of a person's name and hear how wrong it sounds."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: nothing left for the article to do",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Veo a María. — not a la María. Vivo en España. — not en la España."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A proper noun — a name, a country, a city — already points at exactly one thing in the world. The article exists to narrow a noun down to one, and here there is nothing left to narrow. So it goes."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That is the rule you have been following since the first time you said Me llamo…, and it has never been stated."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "culture-pragmatics",
          "title": "Why it's said this way",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Now the part that catches people, and it follows from the same logic."
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "",
                ""
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 2,
              "utterances": [
                "Hablo con el profesor García., talking about him — article.",
                "Profesor García, ¿puedo hablar?, talking to him — none."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A title is not a name. Profesor is an ordinary noun, so it takes an article like any other — until you turn and address the person, at which point you are not identifying them for anybody. You are calling them. And a vocative takes no article, exactly as it takes no personal a."
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Same rule underneath: the article says which one, and it appears precisely when that job needs doing."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "Veo a María, Vivo en España",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *Veo a María*, *Vivo en España*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "el profesor García, then Profesor García, …",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *el profesor García*, then *Profesor García, …*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "repeat",
              "times": 2,
              "source": "[REPEAT x2]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C66-nombres-propios-name",
              "prompt": "Say: I see Maria.",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-PROPER-NOUN-NO-ARTICLE"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 12,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "veo a maria",
                "veo a maria."
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Personal a, but no article.",
                "incorrect": "Veo a Maria -- no la."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C66-nombres-propios-why",
              "prompt": "Why does a name take no article?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-PROPER-NOUN-NO-ARTICLE"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "it already points at one thing",
                "there is nothing left to narrow down"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "The article has no work to do.",
                "incorrect": "It already points at exactly one thing."
              }
            },
            {
              "kind": "activity",
              "scored": true,
              "id": "ES-C66-nombres-propios-title",
              "prompt": "You are speaking TO Professor Garcia. Article or no article?",
              "assesses": [
                "ES-GRAMMAR-TITLE-TAKES-ARTICLE"
              ],
              "responseSeconds": 14,
              "acceptedResponses": [
                "no article",
                "none, it is a vocative"
              ],
              "feedback": {
                "correct": "Addressing, not identifying.",
                "incorrect": "None -- you are calling him, not identifying him."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Why do names take no article? (They already point at one thing.)"
            },
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And when does a title lose its article? (When you are addressing the person.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
